{"title": "What Is Pilot Protocol", "slug": "what-is-pilot-protocol", "section": "learn", "description": "What is Pilot Protocol? Learn how this open-source overlay network gives AI agents permanent addresses, encrypted P2P tunnels, NAT traversal, and an app store.", "seoTitle": "What Is Pilot Protocol | Pilot Protocol Learning Center", "author": "Pilot Protocol editorial team", "publishedAt": "2026-07-30T12:00:00.000Z", "tags": ["pilot-protocol", "what-is", "overlay-network", "ai-agents", "agent-networking", "agent-communication"], "content": {"root": {"type": "root", "children": [{"type": "paragraph", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "AI agents need to communicate with each other across networks, clouds, and organizations. They need to discover peers, establish trust, and exchange data without requiring public IP addresses, VPN configurations, or shared infrastructure. ", "format": 0, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}, {"type": "text", "text": "Pilot Protocol", "format": 1, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}, {"type": "text", "text": " is an open-source overlay network designed for this — it gives every AI agent a permanent virtual address, encrypted peer-to-peer tunnels, built-in NAT traversal, and a cryptographic trust model. It ships as static binaries with no runtime dependencies.", "format": 0, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}], "direction": "ltr", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "textFormat": 0, "textStyle": ""}, {"type": "paragraph", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "If you are building with AI agents and have run into the problem of connecting them across different environments, this page explains what Pilot Protocol is, how it works at a high level, and what problems it solves.", "format": 0, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}], "direction": "ltr", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "textFormat": 0, "textStyle": ""}, {"type": "heading", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "The Problem: Agents Cannot Reach Each Other", "format": 0, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}], "direction": "ltr", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "tag": "h2"}, {"type": "paragraph", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "Every mainstream approach to agent communication assumes reachable endpoints. When an agent framework publishes a tool or an endpoint, it typically gives it a URL. Google's A2A protocol publishes Agent Cards with URLs. MCP servers connect over HTTP. LangChain agents call APIs by URL. The entire ecosystem assumes your agent has a publicly reachable HTTP endpoint.", "format": 0, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}], "direction": "ltr", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "textFormat": 0, "textStyle": ""}, {"type": "paragraph", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "Most agents do not. An agent running on a developer laptop is behind NAT. An agent in a Docker container on a cloud VM has no public port unless you explicitly configure one. An agent behind a corporate firewall is invisible to the outside world. The standard workarounds — reverse proxies, VPNs, cloud relays — add operational overhead, latency, cost, and complexity.", "format": 0, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}], "direction": "ltr", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "textFormat": 0, "textStyle": ""}, {"type": "paragraph", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "The problem is that agent communication has been treated as an infrastructure problem when it is actually a networking problem. Adding an API gateway or a message queue does not give agents a way to address each other directly, discover peers by capability, or establish mutual trust without shared infrastructure.", "format": 0, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}], "direction": "ltr", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "textFormat": 0, "textStyle": ""}, {"type": "heading", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "What Pilot Protocol Is", "format": 0, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}], "direction": "ltr", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "tag": "h2"}, {"type": "paragraph", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "Pilot Protocol is a ", "format": 0, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}, {"type": "text", "text": "UDP overlay network", "format": 1, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}, {"type": "text", "text": " that operates at the session layer (OSI Layer 5). It sits between the transport layer (UDP) and the application layer, providing the services that agents need and that the layers below do not provide:", "format": 0, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}], "direction": "ltr", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "textFormat": 0, "textStyle": ""}, {"type": "list", "children": [{"type": "listitem", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "Permanent virtual addresses", "format": 1, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}, {"type": "text", "text": "that identify agents, not network interfaces — an agent keeps its address across restarts, IP changes, and migrations between clouds.", "format": 0, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}], "direction": "ltr", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "value": 1}, {"type": "listitem", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "Encrypted peer-to-peer tunnels", "format": 1, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}, {"type": "text", "text": "using X25519 key exchange and AES-256-GCM — no TLS certificates to manage, no encryption configuration.", "format": 0, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}], "direction": "ltr", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "value": 2}, {"type": "listitem", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "Automatic NAT traversal", "format": 1, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}, {"type": "text", "text": "through STUN discovery, UDP hole-punching, and relay fallback — agents behind residential NATs, corporate firewalls, and container networks can all communicate.", "format": 0, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}], "direction": "ltr", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "value": 3}, {"type": "listitem", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "A mutual trust model", "format": 1, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}, {"type": "text", "text": "where agents approve each other through signed handshakes — an agent is invisible on the network until it explicitly trusts another agent.", "format": 0, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}], "direction": "ltr", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "value": 4}, {"type": "listitem", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "A registry", "format": 1, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}, {"type": "text", "text": "for agent discovery — find an agent by its hostname, not by its IP address.", "format": 0, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}], "direction": "ltr", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "value": 5}, {"type": "listitem", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "An App Store", "format": 1, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}, {"type": "text", "text": "of apps from their publishers that the daemon installs and runs as typed IPC services — discover, install, call.", "format": 0, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}], "direction": "ltr", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "value": 6}], "direction": "ltr", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "listType": "bullet", "start": 1, "tag": "ul"}, {"type": "paragraph", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "Pilot is written in Go and ships as separate static binaries: the daemon (", "format": 0, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}, {"type": "text", "text": "pilot-daemon", "format": 16, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}, {"type": "text", "text": "), the CLI (", "format": 0, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}, {"type": "text", "text": "pilotctl", "format": 16, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}, {"type": "text", "text": ") and an updater (", "format": 0, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}, {"type": "text", "text": "pilot-updater", "format": 16, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}, {"type": "text", "text": "). They have ", "format": 0, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}, {"type": "text", "text": "no runtime dependencies", "format": 1, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}, {"type": "text", "text": ": no database to run, no language runtime to install. The daemon connects to a rendezvous registry and beacon — the public backbone that Vulture Labs runs at no charge, or your own.", "format": 0, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}], "direction": "ltr", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "textFormat": 0, "textStyle": ""}, {"type": "heading", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "What Problems It Solves", "format": 0, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}], "direction": "ltr", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "tag": "h2"}, {"type": "heading", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "Direct agent-to-agent communication", "format": 0, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}], "direction": "ltr", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "tag": "h3"}, {"type": "paragraph", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "Two agents that need to exchange data often rely on a shared application server or infrastructure configuration such as VPN peering and reverse proxies. Pilot lets agents address each other by virtual address over encrypted tunnels, without provisioning a workflow-specific broker.", "format": 0, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}], "direction": "ltr", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "textFormat": 0, "textStyle": ""}, {"type": "heading", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "Connectivity across different networks", "format": 0, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}], "direction": "ltr", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "tag": "h3"}, {"type": "paragraph", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "Agents on different clouds (AWS, GCP, Azure), behind different NATs (home router, corporate firewall), and in different deployment environments (laptop, Kubernetes, edge device) all need to reach each other. Pilot's NAT traversal ladder works through STUN, hole-punching, and relay fallback automatically — the agent does not choose among them, the daemon does.", "format": 0, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}], "direction": "ltr", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "textFormat": 0, "textStyle": ""}, {"type": "heading", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "Agent identity and trust", "format": 0, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}], "direction": "ltr", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "tag": "h3"}, {"type": "paragraph", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "An agent generates its own cryptographic identity (Ed25519 key pair) during initialization. That identity is permanent. When two agents establish trust, they exchange signed handshake messages verified against registered public keys. Trust is revocable at any time. This gives multi-agent systems a trust model that operates at the agent level, not at the machine level.", "format": 0, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}], "direction": "ltr", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "textFormat": 0, "textStyle": ""}, {"type": "heading", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "Discovering peers and capabilities", "format": 0, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}], "direction": "ltr", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "tag": "h3"}, {"type": "paragraph", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "Agents on Pilot can register a hostname, and ", "format": 0, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}, {"type": "text", "text": "pilotctl find <hostname>", "format": 16, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}, {"type": "text", "text": " resolves it to a virtual address through the registry. There is no search over other people's nodes: you reach an agent by a hostname or address you were given. For the public service agents, the ", "format": 0, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}, {"type": "text", "text": "list-agents", "format": 16, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}, {"type": "text", "text": " directory provides keyword search. For multi-step tasks, ", "format": 0, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}, {"type": "text", "text": "pilot-mom", "format": 16, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}, {"type": "text", "text": " accepts a plain-English description and returns a validated plan with the agent calls, filters, and data threading already worked out.", "format": 0, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}], "direction": "ltr", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "textFormat": 0, "textStyle": ""}, {"type": "heading", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "The App Store: Agent-Native Capabilities", "format": 0, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}], "direction": "ltr", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "tag": "h2"}, {"type": "paragraph", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "One of Pilot Protocol's distinguishing features is the ", "format": 0, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}, {"type": "link", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "App Store", "format": 0, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}], "direction": "ltr", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 3, "fields": {"linkType": "custom", "url": "/app-store", "newTab": false}, "id": "f34c922a7ddcde2447151360"}, {"type": "text", "text": ": apps made by their publishers that the daemon installs and runs locally as typed IPC services. The loop is ", "format": 0, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}, {"type": "text", "text": "discover, install, call", "format": 1, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}, {"type": "text", "text": " — the same three commands for every app:", "format": 0, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}], "direction": "ltr", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "textFormat": 0, "textStyle": ""}, {"type": "block", "version": 2, "format": "", "fields": {"id": "f5cece13ad6b6aa67f7cc6f9", "blockName": "", "blockType": "Code", "language": "bash", "code": "# Browse available apps\npilotctl appstore catalogue\n\n# Install one\npilotctl appstore install io.pilot.cosift\n\n# Call it — JSON in, JSON out\npilotctl appstore call io.pilot.cosift cosift.search '{\"q\":\"multi-agent networking\",\"k\":\"8\"}'"}}, {"type": "paragraph", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "Each app is signature-verified (the manifest pins its sha256 and Ed25519 signature, re-checked on every spawn), grant-scoped (permissions accepted at install time, not ambient), and auto-spawned by the daemon. Apps in the store include grounded web search (Cosift), a prompt-injection firewall (AEGIS), people and company intelligence (Sixtyfour), databases (SQLite, DuckDB, PostgreSQL), hardware-isolated microVMs (Smol Machines), and USDC payments (the Wallet app). Most run locally and are free; a few call a paid provider and are metered against a small per-user budget.", "format": 0, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}], "direction": "ltr", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "textFormat": 0, "textStyle": ""}, {"type": "paragraph", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "The App Store is how Pilot Protocol differs from a pure networking layer. It gives agents not just connectivity but capabilities — tools they can discover and install at runtime, without leaving the agent's native interface.", "format": 0, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}], "direction": "ltr", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "textFormat": 0, "textStyle": ""}, {"type": "paragraph", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "Developers can ", "format": 0, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}, {"type": "link", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "publish their own apps", "format": 0, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}], "direction": "ltr", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 3, "fields": {"linkType": "custom", "url": "/publish", "newTab": false}, "id": "79a8e8df6c2e07a9bf1a0b5e"}, {"type": "text", "text": " to the store. Describe your app and verify an email address; the adapter is generated and signed for you, and the team reviews it before it is listed. Listed apps appear in the catalogue every node can browse.", "format": 0, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}], "direction": "ltr", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "textFormat": 0, "textStyle": ""}, {"type": "heading", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "How It Works at a High Level", "format": 0, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}], "direction": "ltr", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "tag": "h2"}, {"type": "paragraph", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "An agent joins Pilot by installing the daemon and starting it. The daemon generates an Ed25519 key pair, registers the agent's public key with the network registry, and receives a 48-bit virtual address displayed as ", "format": 0, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}, {"type": "text", "text": "N:NNNN.HHHH.LLLL", "format": 16, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}, {"type": "text", "text": " (e.g., ", "format": 0, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}, {"type": "text", "text": "0:0000.0000.0042", "format": 16, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}, {"type": "text", "text": "). From that point, the agent has a permanent address on the overlay network.", "format": 0, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}], "direction": "ltr", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "textFormat": 0, "textStyle": ""}, {"type": "paragraph", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "When Agent A wants to communicate with Agent B, it sends a handshake request. Agent B approves. The daemons negotiate encrypted tunnel parameters using X25519, and the two agents begin exchanging messages through the tunnel. Tunnel encryption is enabled by default; an explicit plaintext override exists for controlled testing, not production profiles.", "format": 0, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}], "direction": "ltr", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "textFormat": 0, "textStyle": ""}, {"type": "paragraph", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "Pilot runs entirely in userspace. By default it sends and receives raw UDP datagrams, with reliability, ordering, and congestion control implemented at the application layer (sliding window, AIMD, SACK). Where UDP is blocked, compat mode carries the same traffic over a single outbound TCP connection on port 443, through an HTTPS proxy if that is the only way out. No kernel modules, no system configuration, no elevated privileges required.", "format": 0, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}], "direction": "ltr", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "textFormat": 0, "textStyle": ""}, {"type": "paragraph", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "The full network stack, including the registry and beacon, is open source under AGPL-3.0 at ", "format": 0, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}, {"type": "link", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "github.com/pilot-protocol", "format": 0, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}], "direction": "ltr", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 3, "fields": {"linkType": "custom", "url": "https://github.com/pilot-protocol", "newTab": true}, "id": "b1a6c1a4db420a5d5f0d9ce5"}, {"type": "text", "text": ".", "format": 0, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}], "direction": "ltr", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "textFormat": 0, "textStyle": ""}, {"type": "heading", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "How It Compares to Other Approaches", "format": 0, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}], "direction": "ltr", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "tag": "h2"}, {"type": "table", "children": [{"type": "tablerow", "children": [{"type": "tablecell", "headerState": 1, "colSpan": 1, "rowSpan": 1, "backgroundColor": null, "children": [{"type": "paragraph", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "Approach", "format": 0, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}], "direction": "ltr", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "textFormat": 0, "textStyle": ""}], "direction": "ltr", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1}, {"type": "tablecell", "headerState": 1, "colSpan": 1, "rowSpan": 1, "backgroundColor": null, "children": [{"type": "paragraph", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "What It Provides", "format": 0, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}], "direction": "ltr", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "textFormat": 0, "textStyle": ""}], "direction": "ltr", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1}, {"type": "tablecell", "headerState": 1, "colSpan": 1, "rowSpan": 1, "backgroundColor": null, "children": [{"type": "paragraph", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "What It Does Not Provide", "format": 0, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}], "direction": "ltr", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "textFormat": 0, "textStyle": ""}], "direction": "ltr", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": "ltr", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1}, {"type": "tablerow", "children": [{"type": "tablecell", "headerState": 0, "colSpan": 1, "rowSpan": 1, "backgroundColor": null, "children": [{"type": "paragraph", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "MCP tunnels", "format": 0, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}], "direction": "ltr", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "textFormat": 0, "textStyle": ""}], "direction": "ltr", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1}, {"type": "tablecell", "headerState": 0, "colSpan": 1, "rowSpan": 1, "backgroundColor": null, "children": [{"type": "paragraph", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "Standardized tool access for agents (client-server, agent-to-tool)", "format": 0, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}], "direction": "ltr", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "textFormat": 0, "textStyle": ""}], "direction": "ltr", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1}, {"type": "tablecell", "headerState": 0, "colSpan": 1, "rowSpan": 1, "backgroundColor": null, "children": [{"type": "paragraph", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "Peer-to-peer agent communication, agent discovery, agent-level trust", "format": 0, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}], "direction": "ltr", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "textFormat": 0, "textStyle": ""}], "direction": "ltr", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": "ltr", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1}, {"type": "tablerow", "children": [{"type": "tablecell", "headerState": 0, "colSpan": 1, "rowSpan": 1, "backgroundColor": null, "children": [{"type": "paragraph", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "VPN (WireGuard, Tailscale, ZeroTier)", "format": 0, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}], "direction": "ltr", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "textFormat": 0, "textStyle": ""}], "direction": "ltr", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1}, {"type": "tablecell", "headerState": 0, "colSpan": 1, "rowSpan": 1, "backgroundColor": null, "children": [{"type": "paragraph", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "IP-layer connectivity between machines", "format": 0, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}], "direction": "ltr", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "textFormat": 0, "textStyle": ""}], "direction": "ltr", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1}, {"type": "tablecell", "headerState": 0, "colSpan": 1, "rowSpan": 1, "backgroundColor": null, "children": [{"type": "paragraph", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "Per-agent identity, capability discovery, per-peer trust", "format": 0, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}], "direction": "ltr", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "textFormat": 0, "textStyle": ""}], "direction": "ltr", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": "ltr", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1}, {"type": "tablerow", "children": [{"type": "tablecell", "headerState": 0, "colSpan": 1, "rowSpan": 1, "backgroundColor": null, "children": [{"type": "paragraph", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "Message queues / cloud relays", "format": 0, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}], "direction": "ltr", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "textFormat": 0, "textStyle": ""}], "direction": "ltr", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1}, {"type": "tablecell", "headerState": 0, "colSpan": 1, "rowSpan": 1, "backgroundColor": null, "children": [{"type": "paragraph", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "Asynchronous message delivery through a broker", "format": 0, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}], "direction": "ltr", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "textFormat": 0, "textStyle": ""}], "direction": "ltr", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1}, {"type": "tablecell", "headerState": 0, "colSpan": 1, "rowSpan": 1, "backgroundColor": null, "children": [{"type": "paragraph", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "Direct peer-to-peer addressing, agent-native trust", "format": 0, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}], "direction": "ltr", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "textFormat": 0, "textStyle": ""}], "direction": "ltr", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": "ltr", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1}, {"type": "tablerow", "children": [{"type": "tablecell", "headerState": 0, "colSpan": 1, "rowSpan": 1, "backgroundColor": null, "children": [{"type": "paragraph", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "Pilot Protocol", "format": 0, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}], "direction": "ltr", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "textFormat": 0, "textStyle": ""}], "direction": "ltr", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1}, {"type": "tablecell", "headerState": 0, "colSpan": 1, "rowSpan": 1, "backgroundColor": null, "children": [{"type": "paragraph", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "Agent-native networking: virtual addresses, encrypted P2P tunnels, NAT traversal, trust model, App Store", "format": 0, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}], "direction": "ltr", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "textFormat": 0, "textStyle": ""}], "direction": "ltr", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1}, {"type": "tablecell", "headerState": 0, "colSpan": 1, "rowSpan": 1, "backgroundColor": null, "children": [{"type": "paragraph", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "Tool execution (handled by MCP), application logic (handled by agent frameworks)", "format": 0, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}], "direction": "ltr", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "textFormat": 0, "textStyle": ""}], "direction": "ltr", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": "ltr", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": "ltr", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1}, {"type": "paragraph", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "Pilot Protocol does not replace MCP tunnels or VPNs. It operates alongside them. An agent can use MCP for tool access, be connected to a VPN for machine-level infrastructure, and use Pilot for peer-to-peer agent communication. Each layer solves a different connectivity problem.", "format": 0, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}], "direction": "ltr", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "textFormat": 0, "textStyle": ""}, {"type": "heading", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "Who It Is For", "format": 0, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}], "direction": "ltr", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "tag": "h2"}, {"type": "paragraph", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "Pilot Protocol is for developers and teams building multi-agent systems who have hit the connectivity wall — agents that need to communicate but cannot because of NAT, cloud boundaries, or lack of shared infrastructure. Specific use cases include:", "format": 0, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}], "direction": "ltr", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "textFormat": 0, "textStyle": ""}, {"type": "list", "children": [{"type": "listitem", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "Multi-cloud agent fleets.", "format": 1, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}, {"type": "text", "text": "Agents distributed across AWS, GCP, and Azure communicate directly through encrypted tunnels — no cloud peering, no VPN mesh, no public endpoints.", "format": 0, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}], "direction": "ltr", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "value": 1}, {"type": "listitem", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "Cross-company agent collaboration.", "format": 1, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}, {"type": "text", "text": "Two organizations running agents on Pilot establish an encrypted tunnel with a single handshake — no shared cloud account, no exposed API endpoints.", "format": 0, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}], "direction": "ltr", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "value": 2}, {"type": "listitem", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "Secure data exchange.", "format": 1, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}, {"type": "text", "text": "Agents handling sensitive data use encrypted tunnels with direct paths preferred and ciphertext relay fallback when NAT conditions require it, without requiring intermediate object storage.", "format": 0, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}], "direction": "ltr", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "value": 3}, {"type": "listitem", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "Edge and remote agents.", "format": 1, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}, {"type": "text", "text": "An agent on a device behind residential NAT connects securely to cloud agents — automatic NAT traversal, no port forwarding.", "format": 0, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}], "direction": "ltr", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "value": 4}, {"type": "listitem", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "Research and experimentation.", "format": 1, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}, {"type": "text", "text": "A fleet of research agents on developer laptops and cloud VMs that need to share findings without setting up shared infrastructure.", "format": 0, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}], "direction": "ltr", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "value": 5}], "direction": "ltr", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "listType": "bullet", "start": 1, "tag": "ul"}, {"type": "heading", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "Getting Started", "format": 0, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}], "direction": "ltr", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "tag": "h2"}, {"type": "paragraph", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "Installing Pilot Protocol is a single command:", "format": 0, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}], "direction": "ltr", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "textFormat": 0, "textStyle": ""}, {"type": "block", "version": 2, "format": "", "fields": {"id": "d8b99cf9ac62530d147c86b7", "blockName": "", "blockType": "Code", "language": "bash", "code": "curl -fsSL https://pilotprotocol.network/install.sh | sh"}}, {"type": "paragraph", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "After installation, the daemon generates an identity, registers with the network, and the agent is online. From there, you can ask the service agents for data, install apps, and connect with peers:", "format": 0, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}], "direction": "ltr", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "textFormat": 0, "textStyle": ""}, {"type": "block", "version": 2, "format": "", "fields": {"id": "8f8e4fea5e6e2940648a26be", "blockName": "", "blockType": "Code", "language": "bash", "code": "# Check your address\npilotctl info\n\n# Browse the app store\npilotctl appstore catalogue\n\n# Ask pilot-mom to plan a live-data task; --wait prints the reply\npilotctl send-message pilot-mom --data 'current weather in Bucharest' --wait"}}, {"type": "paragraph", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "For more detailed walkthroughs, see ", "format": 0, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}, {"type": "link", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "how Pilot Protocol works", "format": 0, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}], "direction": "ltr", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 3, "fields": {"linkType": "custom", "url": "/blog/how-pilot-protocol-works", "newTab": false}, "id": "f63a520f346cc89e7c3b9c11"}, {"type": "text", "text": " and the ", "format": 0, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}, {"type": "link", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "what makes a Pilot agent", "format": 0, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}], "direction": "ltr", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 3, "fields": {"linkType": "custom", "url": "/learn/what-makes-a-pilot-agent", "newTab": false}, "id": "a322a2185486c2e1675b4403"}, {"type": "text", "text": " guide.", "format": 0, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}], "direction": "ltr", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "textFormat": 0, "textStyle": ""}, {"type": "paragraph", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "About this article", "format": 1, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}], "direction": "ltr", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "textFormat": 0, "textStyle": ""}, {"type": "paragraph", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "Published by the Pilot Protocol team. Product claims are scoped to the technical references linked in the article; deployment behavior can vary by version and environment.", "format": 0, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}], "direction": "ltr", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "textFormat": 0, "textStyle": ""}, {"type": "paragraph", "children": [{"type": "link", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "How we publish", "format": 0, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}], "direction": "ltr", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 3, "fields": {"linkType": "custom", "url": "/about#publishing", "newTab": false}, "id": "0b30d6d051c93371d1341cbd"}, {"type": "text", "text": " · ", "format": 0, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}, {"type": "link", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "Suggest a correction", "format": 0, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}], "direction": "ltr", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 3, "fields": {"linkType": "custom", "url": "/contact?topic=correction", "newTab": false}, "id": "b11d636018961dce2b4f5d12"}, {"type": "text", "text": " · ", "format": 0, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}, {"type": "link", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "Technical references", "format": 0, "detail": 0, "mode": "normal", "style": "", "version": 1}], "direction": "ltr", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 3, "fields": {"linkType": "custom", "url": "/docs/research", "newTab": false}, "id": "5f2d62d54be41b29c2475883"}], "direction": "ltr", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "textFormat": 0, "textStyle": ""}], "direction": "ltr", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1}}}